Title: "Set Constructions"
Id: "b49297d0-a6d3-4843-85a0-2241c30b5a05"


Text: "Equipped with the core axioms of Zermelo--Fraenkel theory, we can now
       define the canonical elementary constructors of mathematics.

       The simplest non-trivial set formed from an existing entity $X$ is its
       *singleton set*, denoted $\{X\}$, whose solitary member is $X$. The
       existence of $\{X\}$ follows immediately from the Axiom of Pairing by
       setting $Y = X$, so that $\{X\} = \{X, X\}$. It is conceptually vital to
       maintain the ontological distinction between an element $X$ and its
       singleton collection $\{X\}$: while $X \in \{X\}$, they are never
       identical (indeed, the Axiom of Foundation forbids $X = \{X\}$ since that
       would imply $X \in X$)."
Id: "032576ab-7fc3-4075-b24a-cd06583d18cd"


[\singleton.set:of{X}]
Defines: Y is \set
using: U is \set
when: X "in" U
expresses:
. let: Z is \set
  then:
  . have: Z "in"? Y
    iff: Z = X
Documented:
. called: "The singleton set of $X?$"
. written: "\left \{ X? \right \}"
. description: "The singleton $\{X\}$ is the set whose sole member is $X$: a set
                $Z$ belongs to it exactly when $Z = X$. Its existence follows
                from pairing applied to $X$ with itself."
Id: "08f35257-d0a7-42af-9866-7d138c56fc4c"


Text: "Applying the Axiom of Pairing to two distinct sets $x$ and $y$ produces
       the *unordered pair* $\{x, y\}$. By the Axiom of Extensionality, the
       identity of $\{x, y\}$ depends solely on its elements, which implies
       $\{x, y\} = \{y, x\}$: the order of listing carries no mathematical
       significance."
Id: "c128a6a8-b892-4177-8622-29ba3cfa473a"


[\unordered.pair:of{x, y}]
Defines: Z is \set
using: U is \set
when: x, y "in" U
expresses:
. let: z is \set
  then:
  . have: z "in"? Z
    iff: (.z = x.) \.or./ (.z = y.)
Documented:
. called: "unordered pair of $x?$ and $y?$"
. written: "\left \{ x?, y? \right \}"
. description: "The unordered pair $\{x, y\}$ is the set whose members are
                precisely $x$ and $y$."
Id: "17401045-fc72-4bd4-af7d-ab578690f81c"


Text: "The *successor* of a set $X$, denoted $S(X) = X \cup \{X\}$, adjoins $X$
       itself as a new member to the elements already in $X$.

       This elegant construction, introduced by John von Neumann in 1923, forms
       the engine for encoding the natural numbers within pure set theory:
       $$\begin{aligned}
       0 &:= \emptyset \\
       1 &:= S(0) = \emptyset \cup \{\emptyset\} = \{\emptyset\} = \{0\} \\
       2 &:= S(1) = \{0\} \cup \{1\} = \{0, 1\} \\
       3 &:= S(2) = \{0, 1\} \cup \{2\} = \{0, 1, 2\}
       \end{aligned}$$
       In this von Neumann ordinal representation, each natural number $n$ is
       literally the set of all preceding natural numbers, so that
       $n = \{0, 1, \dots, n-1\}$ and the cardinality $|n|$ is precisely $n$."
Id: "f49b05f5-b826-45d0-b327-b248946e28fc"


[\set.successor:of{X}]
Defines: Y is \set
when: X is \set
expresses:
. let: Z is \set
  then:
  . have: Z "in"? Y
    iff:
    . (.Z "in"? X.) \.or./ (.Z = X.)
Documented:
. called: "The set successor of $X?$"
. written: "X? \cup \left \{ X? \right \}"
. description: "The successor of $X$ is the set $X \cup \{X\}$, whose members
                are the members of $X$ together with $X$ itself. Starting from
                $\emptyset$ and repeating this step generates the natural
                numbers."
Id: "002b9914-ad06-4f5a-a3fb-803bd86e6c4b"


SectionTitle: "Set-Builder Notation"
Id: "55851b31-9fa9-4b8e-b61b-315b7559c66d"


Text: "In mathematical practice, sets are rarely specified by exhaustive
       enumeration. Instead, they are defined through *comprehension* (or
       set-builder) notation, which specifies a predicate characterizing
       membership: $$\{x \in A : \varphi(x)\}$$ Here $\varphi$ is a predicate on
       sets, and the Axiom Schema of Separation guarantees that the collection
       of all elements in the preexisting set $A$ satisfying $\varphi$ forms a
       legitimate set. For finite sets, we retain the familiar roster notation
       $\{x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n\}$."
Id: "b218d7dd-c01a-476d-9975-b05de57b8b8f"


[\set.property(X_)]
Declares: phi(X_) is (_ is \set) -> (_ is \\statement)
Documented:
. called: "set property"
. description: "A set property is a predicate on sets: given a set it yields a
                statement, true or false. Properties are what the specification
                axiom uses to carve subsets out of existing sets."
Id: "cc680a81-3338-4848-8b9d-fcf368dd759e"


[\set:of{x[i_ := 1...n]}]
Defines: X is \set
when: x[1...n] is \\anything
expresses:
. let: y is \\anything
  then:
  . have: y "in"? X
    iff:
    . anyOf:
      . x[1...n] = y
Documented:
. called: "set of $\left \{ x? \right \}$"
. written: "\left \{ x? \right \}"
. description: "The set $\{x_1, \dots, x_n\}$ listed by its elements: a value
                belongs to it exactly when it equals one of the listed $x_i$.
                This is the finite, roster form of set-builder notation."
Id: "61c901bc-5f36-479b-a32c-c37b7f98a9fb"


SectionTitle: "Ordered Pairs and Products"
Id: "b61f3f41-454a-4ea1-a69c-35fe7fb2ceb3"


Text: "While sets are inherently unordered collections, mathematics
       fundamentally requires ordered structures where the first coordinate can
       be distinguished from the second—such as geometric coordinates $(x, y)$,
       directed graphs, relations, and input-output pairings of functions.

       In 1921, Kazimierz Kuratowski solved the problem of encoding ordered
       pairs within pure set theory by defining:
       $$(x, y) := \{\{x\}, \{x, y\}\}$$ This ingenious device encodes the first
       coordinate $x$ by placing it in a singleton, while the pair $\{x, y\}$
       bundles both coordinates together. The first coordinate can be uniquely
       recovered as the sole element in the intersection of all elements of the
       pair: $\bigcap (x, y) = \{x\}$."
Id: "da7d91b3-693b-4ca2-ac2a-e94555b18c5f"


[\pair:of{x, y}]
Defines: p := \set:of{\set:of{x}, \set:of{x, y}} is \set
when: x, y is \\anything
Documented:
. called: "pair of $x?$ and $y?$"
. written: "\langle x?, y? \rangle"
. description: "The ordered pair $\langle x, y \rangle$, encoded as the set
                $\{\{x\}, \{x, y\}\}$. This encoding lets the first and second
                components be recovered from the set alone, so order is
                meaningful even though sets are unordered."
Id: "8fd9d494-2882-4639-bb50-44a56626d1c4"


Text: "With ordered pairs established, we can define the *Cartesian product*
       $A \times B$, named in honor of René Descartes. The Cartesian product
       gathers all ordered pairs $(a, b)$ such that $a \in A$ and $b \in B$:
       $$A \times B := \{(a, b) : a \in A \land b \in B\}$$ The Cartesian
       product is the foundational arena of modern mathematics: binary relations
       on $A$ are defined as subsets of $A \times A$, and functions from $A$ to
       $B$ are defined as special subsets of $A \times B$."
Id: "331daec2-edc7-4316-8fb6-0c801c3692ed"


[A \.set.cross./ B]
Defines: X := \set@{(a_, b_) : a_ "in" A; b_ "in" B}
when: A, B is \set
Documented:
. called: "Cartesian product of $A?$ and $B?$"
. written: "A? \times B?"
. description: "The Cartesian product $A \times B$ is the set of all ordered
                pairs $(a, b)$ with $a \in A$ and $b \in B$. Relations on a set
                are subsets of its product with itself, and functions are
                relations of a special kind."
Id: "3bdd9cfb-f836-4d44-880a-873bb772cdef"


Theorem:
given: x, y, u, v is \set
then: \pair:of{x, y} = \pair:of{u, v}
iff:
. x = u
. y = v
Documented:
. called: "coordinate equality for ordered pairs"
. description: "Kuratowski ordered pairs are equal exactly when their
                corresponding coordinates are equal."
Proof: """If $x = u$ and $y = v$, then
          $\{\{x\}, \{x, y\}\} = \{\{u\}, \{u, v\}\}$ trivially. Conversely,
          suppose $\{\{x\}, \{x, y\}\} = \{\{u\}, \{u, v\}\}$. The intersection
          of all members of the left set is $\{x\}$, and of the right set is
          $\{u\}$, which forces $\{x\} = \{u\}$ and hence $x = u$. Now consider
          two cases:
          1. If $x = y$, then the left set is $\{\{x\}, \{x\}\} = \{\{x\}\}$,
             meaning the right set must also be a singleton. Thus
             $\{u\} = \{u, v\}$, which forces $u = v$, yielding $x = y = u = v$.
          2. If $x \neq y$, then the left set contains a unique two-element
             member $\{x, y\}$, which must equal the unique two-element member
             $\{u, v\}$ on the right. Since $x = u$ and $x \neq y$, it follows
             that $y = v$."""
Id: "31691bb2-e8e1-4218-be4f-759add2b7ab7"


Theorem:
given: A, B, C is \set
then:
. exists: phi(x__) is \function:on{(.(.A \.set.cross./ B.) \.set.cross./ C.)}:to{(.A \.set.cross./ (.B \.set.cross./ C.).)}
  suchThat:
  . phi is? \(bijective)::function:?on{(.(.A \.set.cross./ B.) \.set.cross./ C.)}:?to{(.A \.set.cross./ (.B \.set.cross./ C.).)}
  . forAll:
    . a "in" A
    . b "in" B
    . c "in" C
    then: phi(((a, b), c)) = (a, (b, c))
Documented:
. called: "Canonical Associativity Bijection for Cartesian Products"
. description: "The map $((a,b),c)\mapsto(a,(b,c))$ is a canonical bijection
                from $(A\times B)\times C$ to $A\times(B\times C)$. The two
                products are therefore naturally identifiable but are not
                literally equal under the ordered-pair encoding."
Proof: """Define $\varphi : (A \times B) \times C \to A \times (B \times C)$ by
          $\varphi(((a, b), c)) = (a, (b, c))$. To show $\varphi$ is injective:
          if $\varphi(((a_1, b_1), c_1)) = \varphi(((a_2, b_2), c_2))$, then
          $(a_1, (b_1, c_1)) = (a_2, (b_2, c_2))$. By coordinate equality of
          ordered pairs, $a_1 = a_2$ and $(b_1, c_1) = (b_2, c_2)$, which
          further implies $b_1 = b_2$ and $c_1 = c_2$. Thus
          $((a_1, b_1), c_1) = ((a_2, b_2), c_2)$. To show $\varphi$ is
          surjective: for any $(a, (b, c)) \in A \times (B \times C)$, the
          element $((a, b), c) \in (A \times B) \times C$ maps directly to
          $(a, (b, c))$ under $\varphi$. Hence $\varphi$ is a bijection."""
Id: "cd332b77-e7cc-4e59-92e6-40decc503be8"
